# Framework overview

Read this before stage or execution work. It gives each stage's inputs, authority, and obligations without loading every stage skill. All seven source copies stay byte-identical; compare them after editing one.

The pipeline reduces ambiguity in layers. Each stage reads the directories above it and writes exactly one directory of its own, all under `.constitution/` at the repository root. Facts something reads mechanically live in YAML validated by JSON Schemas; prose lives in markdown, with front matter where one fact must be read. The `constitution-tooling` skill ships the schemas, the CLI that validates, renders, migrates, and reconciles, and `references/layout.md`, which names every file, its schema, and its writer.

| Stage | Skill | Reads | Writes | Decides |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| 1 | `defining-product-requirements` | User intent, the codebase | `constitution.yaml` archetype, `prd/` | What to build, for whom, and why |
| 2 | `designing-solution-architecture` | `prd/` | `architecture/` | The logical shape of the system |
| 3 | `specifying-technical-implementation` | `prd/`, `architecture/`, the code | `tech-spec/` | The technical decisions, and where each artifact lives in the code |
| 4 | `planning-engineering-execution` | `prd/`, `architecture/`, `tech-spec/` | `tasks/`, `constitution.yaml` concurrency | The epic graph and its tickets |

The optional `interview` skill runs before any of the four stages. It writes an interview record, owns the open-decisions register, and never writes a stage directory. It can close a question on the spot with a bounded spike or commission a design review the user rules from; a stage that hits a question only evidence can answer sends the user back to it. Its Realign target audits a running project against the whole constitution and schedules Evolution passes down the pipeline.

Every stage ends with valid output and reports warnings; reuse validation whose inputs are unchanged. Small related stage changes and patches can share one coherent pull request, reviewed by `pr-review-fix-loop` in `documents` mode when it changes only constitution documents. Keep each stage's decision ownership and useful commit boundaries; no stage or commit needs its own pull request.

Stage 3 has two full-stage pass types. The decisions pass, before Stage 4, chooses the stack and writes the ADRs, guidelines, and sidecars pointing at where each contract and data-model artifact will live in the code. The alignment pass, after a wave merges, records what the code settled and puts the decisions execution made in front of the user. A bounded `pass: execution-patch` repairs authorized physical drift during execution and returns changes and verification to the epic lead, who owns the separate spec commit and independent review; reconciliation handles shared bookkeeping. Raw artifacts such as SQL, migrations, OpenAPI, and `.proto` files live in the code, never under `.constitution/`; `tech-spec/` holds decisions and pointers, not a second copy.

`validate` accepts a constitution with only the stages written so far: an absent `architecture/` after a greenfield Stage 1 pass is not an error, and coverage checks that need a later stage run once that stage is present. A later stage without its upstream is an error. `init --stage architecture` (or a later stage name) writes that stage's empty files.

## After planning

Execution skills have separate write authority: they read the constitution, and only reconciliation writes its shared parts.

| Skill | Role | Reads | Writes |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `execute-epic-end-to-end` | Epic lead: {% if harness.id == "grok" %}designs its own plan in a step-0 plan-mode session when standalone (or skips plan mode when the plan is already approved), writes the repo plan file after exit, or executes{% elif harness.id == "codex" %}writes its own plan file in step 0 when standalone, or executes{% else %}writes its own plan file in a step-0 plan-mode session when standalone, or executes{% endif %} the wave lead's plan file when orchestrated; then runs without questions, dispatching a fresh worker per milestone, verifying (including live-verification recipes), committing, getting each milestone reviewed, opening the pull request, and running its review loop to clean | The epic file, the plan file when orchestrated, `critical-path.md`, `tech-spec/`, `architecture/`, `prd/`, the open-decisions register | Milestone commits; the epic's own file (status, deviations, discovered decisions, closeout); `evidence/` manifests |
| Worker (dispatched by the lead) | Implements one bounded milestone, including routine formatting, types, and focused verification; never reused for another milestone | The assigned outcome, exact revision, named ticket and spec pointers, constraints, and required evidence | Code, tests, config; a spike file for a `Spike` ticket; nothing else under `.constitution/` |
| Spec-patch worker (dispatched by the lead) | Answers one physical drift report | The drift report, the named tech-spec files | Only those `tech-spec/` files, in a commit with no code |
| `reviewing-milestone-commits` | Independent review of one committed milestone | The commit, surrounding code, the constitution, `validate` output | Nothing |
| `execute-wave` | Wave lead: {% if harness.id == "grok" %}designs every epic in the wave in one step-0 plan-mode session and writes the repo plan file or files after exit;{% elif harness.id == "codex" %}plans every epic in the wave outside the constitution in step 0;{% else %}plans every epic in the wave outside the constitution in one step-0 plan-mode session;{% endif %} makes cross-epic judgments, dispatches epic leads, tracks native handles, merges eligible pull requests in the approved order, then reconciles and derives | `critical-path.md` waves, `constitution.yaml` concurrency, the epic files, the project's agent instructions | Plan files outside `.constitution/`; nothing in the constitution until reconciliation |
| `reconcile` (a `constitution-tooling` command) | Applies closeout deltas and fast-lane commits after the pull requests merge, as one commit on the default branch | The closeouts, git history and its commit trailers | `tasks/completed/`, `critical-path.md`, `tasks/changelog.yaml`, `tech-spec/changelog.yaml`, verification-command flags, settled records, the register's proposed entries and already approved rulings, a routing report that carries the code decisions to Stage 3 |
